

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Due dates only. Good — the scope just collapsed from "a notifications system" to something much smaller, and I want to
  name the constraint that decides the last piece.

  [1mA page can't notify you when it isn't open.[0m Real background delivery — a notification that reaches you while the
  browser is closed — needs a service worker [3mplus[0m a push server, which means the backend and accounts you just ruled
  out. Anything we build here fires only while the tab is open. Given that, the honest options:

  - [1mIn-app only[0m — overdue and due-soon tasks are visually surfaced in the list itself (a "Due soon" grouping, overdue
    styling, a count). No permission prompt, no OS integration, works every time the page loads. Costs nothing and can't
    fail silently. What it rules out: nothing reaches you when you're not looking at the app.
  - [1mBrowser Notification API[0m — an OS-level toast on top of the in-app treatment. Buys you a nudge while the tab is open
    in the background. Costs a permission prompt, and it's genuinely unreliable: denied permission, focused-tab
    suppression, and OS Do Not Disturb all silently drop it. Cheap to add later on top of in-app.
  - [1mReal background push[0m — reinstates the server and accounts. Not recommended; it re-buys the whole multi-user cost for
    one feature.

  [1mMy recommendation: in-app only.[0m It delivers the actual value (you open your tasks and immediately see what's due), and
  the browser toast is a strictly additive layer we can bolt on afterward without redesign — so choosing it now costs
  nothing later.
